Not really, I think.

You can roleplay NetHack, but there's nothing to keep you from not doing
so.

For example, Raisse is very strict in roleplaying, normally. She never
plays chaotics, and usually, if I get her right, refrains from things
that would be against a character's alignment.

Others don't care at all about alignment, or break it when it's
convenient only. Me for example, if I play a monk, I'll happily eat a
floating eye and take the minor alignment hit. I'll kill with a samurai
if I really need that peaceful dwarf's pick axe, but I won't poison a
weapon.

Whatever I do in this regard, the game has a small alignment penalty, so
I am already being punished for it. So the Dev Team obviously condones
it, or they would have either made the penalty bigger or made it
impossible at all.

For example, for monks, they could have made them physically incapable
of eating meat. They've been raised on a meatless diet, so their bowels
aren't used to meat anymore. Let them throw up the moment they ingest
anything fleshy. Since this is not implemented, we must assume that
eating meat is only discouraged in a small way, but not forbidden.

